<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Learning Targets and Quizzes</title>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Goals</title>

            <p>
                There are a number of basic skills that you should demonstrate
                fluency with in an introduction to proofs course.
                These checkpoints are intended to emphasize a few things.
                One is that learning is a process.
                You will not be penalized for not demonstrating fluency with a
                skill the first time, but you are expected to eventually get
                there.
                Another is that fluency with basic skills and knowing
                definitions is an important part of doing mathematics.
                For example, you shouldn't expect to be able to complete a
                complex proof about the notion of divides if you don't know the
                definition of divides.
                These checkpoints also provide individual assessment so you know
                whether or not you are keeping up with course material.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Structure</title>

            <p>
                We will have a closed book/closed note checkpoint at the end of
                the first class meeting of the week assessing the following
                learning targets.
                While each target will be graded as only either <q>S</q> for
                Satisfactory or <q>NY</q> for does Not Yet meet the standard.
                Each target appears on 3 consecutive checkpoints.
                Once you have earned an <q>S</q> on a target you do not need to
                do so again.
                Blackboard will be updated weekly to reflect your <q>S</q> s and
                <q>NY</q> s.
                You will be notified which targets will be on a checkpoint, but
                it is your responsibility to know which targets you have yet to
                earn satisfactory.
                Each target will be on three checkpoints.
                Your grade for this component will be the percentage of the 20
                targets listed below that you have earned an S on.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Learning Targets</title>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Come to office hours to ask a question or attend a
                            math event.
                            (See the math major emails for available math
                            events, and let me know if you don’t get it!)
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            State precisely the definition of an even and odd
                            integer and outline the proof of a statement using
                            these terms.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Given a statement to be proven using a direct proof,
                            identify the assumptions to be made and the
                            statements to be proven.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Identify if a given set of numbers is closed under a
                            given operation.
                            
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            State the definition of ``a divides b'' and ``a is
                            congruent to b modulo n,'' and correctly apply these
                            definitions in examples.
                            
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Create and translate sets using set builder notation
                            and fluently work with sets written in set builder
                            notation.
                           
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Use the symbols related to sets (like <m>\in</m>,
                            <m>\notin</m>, <m>\subseteq</m>, <m>\subset</m>, <m>=</m>, <m>\nsubseteq</m>)
                            correctly.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Given two sets and a universal set identify the
                            union, intersection, complement, and set difference
                            and find the power set of a given set.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Identify situations in which it is appropriate to
                            use induction and state the procedure for proving a
                            statement by induction.
                         
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Clearly and correctly disprove a statement using a
                            counterexample.
                           
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Evaluate if a given proof is valid and adheres to
                            our writing guidelines.
                         
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Construct truth tables for statements that use the
                            logical operators and, or, not, and implies, and
                            make conclusions about the truth values of these
                            statements.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Given a conditional statement, identify the
                            hypothesis and conclusion, determine its truth
                            value, and apply it.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Negate a statement with and, or, not, implies,
                            exists, and/or for all.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Given a statement to be proven using a proof by
                            contrapositive, identify the assumptions to be made
                            and the statements to be proven.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Given a statement to be proven using a proof by
                            contradiction, identify the assumptions to be made
                            and the statements to be proven.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>